   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Bit of a weird one - my son put the microwave on for 10 minutes with nothing in it by accident. Now there's a horrible smell. Is it broken?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "⚠ Stop using this appliance\nA burning or hot-plastic smell, smoke, scorching or melting can mean an electrical fault or a fire risk. Stop using it, switch it off and unplug it (or turn it off at the fuse box), and don't run it again — not even as a test — until it has been checked by a qualified engineer. It is likely not broken, as running a microwave empty causes overheating and burning smells but rarely damages the magnetron. However, you must unplug it immediately and let it cool down completely before using it again to prevent fire or permanent damage. From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
